# Copy this to <workspace>/builds/<catalogue>/builds.yaml and make it yours.
# `spangap make-builds`, run in that directory, builds every entry here and
# writes index.html + timestamp beside them; the flasher page serves the
# directory as `../builds/<catalogue>/` and reads both.
#
# `stable` is the catalogue the page starts on; an attached device's own
# `build: catalogue` line, `?build=<catalogue>` and the settings panel's Build
# selector each move it. A catalogue directory holding a file named `.unlisted`
# still builds and is still reachable by name — it is only left out of the tree's
# own index.html, and so out of the selector's list.
#
# flashmon is a generic installer; this file is the ONLY thing that brands it and
# lists its images, so each deployment ships its own.
#
#   project:  the brand shown throughout the UI. Every place the tool would say
#             its own name ("<project> Flasher", the flash button, the default
#             device hostname) uses this, so your deployment reads as its own
#             product rather than "flashmon". It also slugs the image names.
#   url:      where THIS deployment is served.
#   builds:   the catalogue of ready-to-flash images. Each entry is:
#               name        the image name, and its distribution identity — the
#                           device reports it back as sys.build.dist, so a
#                           re-flash offer can mean "same dist, newer stamp".
#                           Free-format: a board may have several entries that
#                           differ in what is left out to fit its flash. Name it
#                           after the hw-<straddle> the detector reports so the
#                           browser can match a detected board to its image;
#                           `generic` is the fallback for any board without a
#                           dedicated image.
#               invocation  the `spangap build` arguments that produce it.
#               image       optional. A photo of the board, shown in the device
#                           box when a detection run identifies it: a path in the
#                           web root (devices/<board>.jpg by convention — see
#                           devices/README.md), never a remote URL. Left out, or
#                           pointing at a file that isn't there, the box is
#                           simply text-only.
#               onboarding  optional. `device` says this image sets a fresh node
#                           up from its own screen, so the flasher asks for
#                           nothing after writing it — it says so and stops.
#                           Two surfaces asking for the same password at the same
#                           moment is a race whose winner nobody can predict, and
#                           a board with a panel in the operator's hands is the
#                           better of the two. Anything else, and the absence of
#                           the key, means the flasher asks: the safe default,
#                           since a build that cannot ask for itself and is never
#                           asked is a node nobody set up. It lands in the
#                           generated index.html as data-onboarding on that
#                           image's link.
#
# Nothing writes this file. What was built, and when, lives in the index.html
# `spangap make-builds` leaves next to the images.
#
# Order matters: each build rewrites the workspace's remembered target
# (.spangap-build), so the LAST entry is what a later bare `spangap build`
# resolves to.
project: MyProject
url: https://myproject.example/flashmon/
builds:
  - name: hw-lilygo-tdeck
    image: devices/hw-lilygo-tdeck.jpg
    onboarding: device            # it has a screen; it asks for itself
    invocation: myorg/myproject --with spangap/hw-lilygo-tdeck
  - name: generic
    invocation: myorg/myproject
